We may refer to elements of $	au_{-1}$ as {\em atoms/} from time to time, though they are certainly not atomic in terms of the metatheory. A {\em litter/} is a subset of $	au_{-1}$ of the form $L_{
u, eta,\gamma} = \{(
u, eta,\gamma,\alpha):\alpha<\kappa\}$. The litters make up a partition of type $-1$ (which is of size $\mu$) into size $\kappa$ sets. On each litter $L = L_{
u, eta,\gamma}$ define a well-ordering $\leq_L$: $(
u, eta,\gamma,\alpha) \leq_L (
u, eta,\gamma,\alpha')$ iff $\alpha\leq \alpha'$. The strict well-ordering $<_L$ is defined in the obvious way. This well-ordering is used in only one place in the paper (definition ef{def:approx_star}), and its use could easily be avoided, but we find its concreteness appealing. A {\em near-litter/} is a subset of $	au_{-1}$ with small symmetric difference from a litter. We define $M \sim N$ as $|M \Delta N|<\kappa$, for $M,N$ near-litters: in English, we say that $M$ is {\em near/} $N$ iff $M \sim N$. Note that nearness is an equivalence relation on near-litters. We define $N^\circ$, for $N$ a near-litter, as the (necessarily unique) litter $L$ such that $L \sim N$.

First, let $\iota_0$ be any injection from the set of litters into $\mu$. Next, use lemma ef{lem:position_generator}, with $X$ taken to be the collection of all singletons of atoms, setting $f(\{a\}) = \{ 
u : 
u \leq \iota_0(L) \}$ where $L$ is the unique litter such that $a \in L$, to create $\iota_1$. Next, use lemma ef{lem:position_generator} again, with $X$ taken to be the collection of all near-litters, setting $$f(N) = \{ 
u : 
u \leq \iota_0(N^\circ) \} \cup  igcup_{a \in N \Delta N^{\circ}}\{ 
u : 
u \leq \iota_1(\{a\})\},$$ to obtain $\iota_2$. Finally, we define a map $\iota$ from near-litters and singletons of atoms to $3 	imes \mu$ by $$ g(x) =  egin{cases} (0, \iota_0(x)) & 	ext{if $x$ is a litter} \ (1, \iota_1(x)) & 	ext{if $x$ is a singleton} \ (2, \iota_2(x)) & 	ext{otherwise} \end{cases} $$ We can then define $\iota_*(x)$ to be the position of $g(x)$ in the restriction of the lexicographic order on $3 	imes \mu$ to the image of $g$ (which is of order type $\mu$). The resulting function $\iota_*$ is then clearly a bijection, and has the required properties by construction, noting that near-litters that happen to be litters will automatically satisfy the third condition.

 ootnote{The use of model elements with support rather than simply model elements as domain elements of the $f$ maps is a substantial contribution of the second author to the mathematics of the paper, above simply verifying the work of the first author. The proof could be carried out without this, but it is much easier to present with this refinement. There are other ways in which the second author has contributed to the mathematics, but this one is especially worthy of note.} We define for each type index $ eta$ less than $\alpha$ and each ordinal $\gamma$ distinct from $ eta$ a function $f_{ eta,\gamma}$ (whose definition does not actually depend on $\alpha$: it will be the same at every stage). $f_{ eta,\gamma}$ is an injection from $	au_ eta^+$ into the set of litters, with range included in $\{L_{
u, eta,\gamma}:
u < \mu\}$ to ensure that distinct $f$ maps have disjoint ranges. When we define $f_{ eta,\gamma}(x)$, we presume that we have already defined it for $y <^+_ eta x$. We define $f_{ eta,\gamma}(x)$ as $L$, where $\iota_*(L)$ is minimal such that  egin{enumerate} \item $L \in \{L_{
u, eta,\gamma}:
u < \mu\}$, \item $\iota^+_*(x) <\iota_*(L)$ [and so for any $N \sim L$, $\iota^+_*(x) <\iota_*(N)$, and for any $z \in L$, $\iota^+_*(x) < \iota_*(\{z\})$], \item and for any $y<_ eta^+ x$, $f_{ eta,\gamma}(y) 
eq L$. \end{enumerate}

Next, use lemma ef{lem:position_generator} again, with $X$ taken to be the collection of all near-litters, setting $$f(N) = \{ 
u : 
u \leq \iota_0(N^\circ) \} \cup  igcup_{a \in N \Delta N^{\circ}}\{ 
u : 
u \leq \iota_1(\{a\})\},$$ to obtain $\iota_2$.
